{"id":"everyday6b-030","label":"stop:economics","verdict":"agree","proposed":null,"sentence":null,"spec":null,"reason":null,"note":"No figure is given, and §5.5 excludes \"naming a budget line ... without an internal figure\". Accepted because the sentence discloses a held-back allowance (contingency) and that the job is only viable because of it, which is margin information, not a bare budget-line name. Close call."}
{"id":"everyday6b-031","label":"stop:economics","verdict":"agree","proposed":null,"sentence":null,"spec":null,"reason":null,"note":null}
{"id":"everyday6b-032","label":"stop:economics","verdict":"agree","proposed":null,"sentence":null,"spec":null,"reason":null,"note":"Roles are muddled (clinic writing to the cleaner). The most coherent reading is that the contract charged is three times the cleaner's quoted cost, i.e. a 3x markup, under \"For internal costing\"; the alternative reading (the clinic's own cost as a ratio) is also §5.5. Accepted, but this is the weakest-written stop."}
